    "evidence_text": "The generator is optimized with maximum likelihood estimation augmented by the discriminator's rewards instead of policy gradient.\n\nOne of the fundamental issues when generating discrete text samples with GANs is training instability.\n\nAt each iteration of adversarial training, we first\n\ntrain the discriminator to assign higher rewards to real data than to generated samples.\n\nOur contributions are mainly as follows:\n\nHowever, MLE tends to generate universal text  .\n\nproposed\n\nOther variants of GANs have been proposed to improve the generator or the discriminator.\n\nInverse reinforcement learning  used a trainable reward approximator as the discriminator to provide dense reward signals at each generation step.\n\nFigure FIGREF3 shows the overview of our model ARAML.\n\nThis training paradigm\n\nDiscriminator\tThe discriminator INLINEFORM0 aims to distinguish real data and generated data like other GANs.\n\nINLINEFORM4 is a temperature hyper-parameter to balance these two terms.\n\nstrategies in the experiment.\n\nrewards.\n\nOur model is also similar to MaliGAN, which gets samples from the generator's distribution.\n\nThe weight of samples INLINEFORM1 .\n\nThe statistics of three datasets\n\nare presented in Table TABREF28 .\n\nIts extension, Seq2Seq, can work on the dialogue dataset  .",
